.TH TLS12 2
.SH NAME
tls12: client, prf, Session \- TLS 1.2 client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "keyring.m";
include "tls12.m";
tls12 := load TLS12 TLS12->PATH;
Session: import tls12;

init:   fn(): string;
prf:    fn(secret: array of byte, label: string,
           seed: array of byte, n: int): array of byte;
client: fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, hostname: string,
           suites: list of int): (ref Session, string);

Session: adt {
    cipher: int;
    certs:  list of array of byte;
    err:    string;
    write:  fn(s: self ref Session, buf: array of byte, n: int): int;
    read:   fn(s: self ref Session, buf: array of byte, n: int): int;
    close:  fn(s: self ref Session);
};
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tls12
is a TLS 1.2 client, built because
.IR ssl3 (2)'s
module speaks only SSLv3 and cannot complete a handshake with any
modern HTTPS host. Its scope is exactly what
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md
measured as sufficient for that plan's real targets: TLS 1.2 only,
ECDHE-RSA key exchange over X25519 only, and the two AEAD cipher
suites
.B TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
.RB ( CS_ECDHE_RSA_AES128_GCM_SHA256 ,
16rC02F) and
.B TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_CHACHA20_POLY1305
.RB ( CS_ECDHE_RSA_CHACHA20_POLY1305 ,
16rCCA8). There is no TLS 1.3, no other curve, no CBC suite, no
session resumption, no renegotiation and no client certificate
support; requests for any of those fail cleanly rather than
negotiating something outside the measured scope.
.PP
The handshake flow, secret derivation and AEAD record construction
are ported from 9front
.RB ( tlshand.c
for the handshake,
.B devtls.c
for the record layer 9front keeps in its kernel), on top of the
primitives
.IR keyring (2)
gained for this plan: HMAC-SHA256, X25519, AES-GCM and
ChaCha20-Poly1305.
.PP
.B Init
must be called once before anything else and returns nil on success
or a diagnostic.
.PP
.B Client
runs a full client handshake over
.IR fd ,
an already-connected TCP stream (normally to port 443), sending
.I hostname
in the SNI extension.
.I Suites
lists the cipher suites to offer in preference order; nil offers both
of the suites above. It returns
.BI (\fIsession\fP,\ nil)
on success or
.BI (nil,\ \fIdiagnostic\fP) .
.PP
An established
.B Session
carries the negotiated
.B cipher
and the server's certificate chain in
.B certs
(DER, leaf first, exactly as received \(em see the warning below).
.B Write
encrypts and sends
.I n
bytes of application data, returning
.I n
or \-1;
.B read
returns up to
.I n
decrypted bytes, 0 at a clean close (the peer's
.B close_notify
alert), or \-1 on error. After any \-1,
.B err
holds a sticky diagnostic and the session is dead.
.B Close
sends a
.B close_notify
alert; closing the file descriptor itself remains the caller's job,
as does dialling (see
.IR dial (2)
\(em and note the connection server gap recorded in
.IR dialtest (1):
in this emu instance names do not resolve, so callers dial literal
IPs while SNI carries the hostname string).
.PP
.B Prf
is RFC 5246 section 5's pseudorandom function (P_SHA256 over
HMAC-SHA256), returning
.I n
bytes derived from
.IR secret ,
.I label
and
.IR seed .
The handshake uses it internally; it stays exported for
.IR tls12prftest (1)
and any other protocol code needing the TLS 1.2 KDF.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/crypt/tls12.b
.br
.B /module/tls12.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tls12test (1),
.IR tls12prftest (1),
.IR keyring (2),
.IR ssl3 (2),
.IR dial (2),
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md
in the source tree.
.SH BUGS
The server's certificate chain is parsed and surfaced but
.B not verified,
and the ServerKeyExchange signature is
.B not checked.
A
.B Session
is therefore encrypted but
.B not authenticated
\(em an active man-in-the-middle can impersonate any server. No code
may present a session as a secure connection, and no man page may
imply it, until the chain-verification work in
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md
lands. That, and the trust-root question it drags in, is the plan's
one remaining open gap.
